From 71069d5ea20bba21561f9ae54e44c19364483ee9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Den Zuko <denzuko@dapla.net>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 09:29:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] doc: document *always-quote*, its compile-time-only behavior

*always-quote* already does the right thing when a template's attribute
values are placeholders for another system to substitute into at
runtime -- e.g. HTML embedded in a SQL string a database fills in with
its own format() at request time. Spinneret only ever sees the
placeholder text at generation time, which typically has no whitespace,
so the default unquoted output is valid for the placeholder and
silently broken the moment something with whitespace lands there later.

Two things about *always-quote* aren't obvious from its one-line
docstring and aren't mentioned in the README at all:

- It has to be set with SETF before the code using it is compiled --
  a LET around the call site has no effect, since whether to quote a
  given attribute is decided at macroexpansion time, not when the
  compiled tag form actually runs. This is easy to get backwards on
  a first read, since most special variables work fine with a
  dynamic LET.
- It exists at all, for this specific failure mode -- there's nothing
  in the README's Validation section (the natural place to look for
  attribute-related behavior) pointing at it.

This adds a short 'Attribute quoting' section to the README describing
the failure mode and both gotchas, and expands the *always-quote*
docstring itself to carry the same warning inline.
---
 README.md    | 50 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 special.lisp | 12 +++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 61 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 2c323e3..57da08a 100644
--- a/README.md
